      "body": "## Overview\n\nRenderers with their own chat encoding (DeepSeek V3.2/V4/V4.1) render an empty conversation to a bare BOS prompt, so `/v1/responses` with `input: []` returns 200 with text generated from nothing. This returns the same 400 that HF-tokenizer models already return.\n\n## Claims\n\n- An empty conversation now returns 400 `BadRequestError` (\"Cannot apply chat template to an empty conversation. Provide at least one message.\") for every renderer.\n- Non-empty conversations are unchanged, including Responses requests with `input: []` plus `instructions` or `previous_response_id`.\n\n## Validation\n\nNew `tests/renderers/test_empty_conversation.py`: the 3 rejection cases fail on `main` and pass with this change; the 2 controls pass on both.\n\n## Details\n\n`transformers` refuses an empty conversation in `apply_chat_template`, but the DeepSeek tokenizers override that method and skip the check. The check now runs at the start of `BaseRenderer.render_chat` and `render_chat_async`, after each endpoint has built its messages. The Rust frontend already rejects an empty conversation (`ChatRequest::validate`); this brings the Python frontend in line.\n\nClaude Code used to create the new tests.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n",
